<b>Interview 29: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;（&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;）： Fuctionary, Jinhua, Zhejiang, male, born in 1950s, in Zhejiang</b>

<p><b>
What do you think of&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;bringing 1001 people to Kassel?</b>

<br>It takes great skill to pull this off, it would be hard for any Chinese artist to do. In the 1930s, the "Mother of the Grand Canal" touched the world, today, &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;'s "Fairytale" uses a touching event to accomplish such an extensive work, he's very skilled. That's my biggest impression. 1001 is a very auspicious number, "A Thousand and One Nights" is a also fairytale. This project is very beautiful, inviting people from all walks of life -many who have never left the country- to cross borders and experience foreign cultures and atmospheres. I think this is essential. 

<p>I am an administrator, specialized in managing private firms. The approach towards service is very good here, shopping is a completely different experience inside and outside of China. Here, their smiles aren't forced, simply innate and spontaneous. That's why I think this Chinese "Fairytale" educates people's character. Fairytales' meanings are exterior to the stories. Our experience here is a fairytale: collective living, like a student's life, still having to wash the dishes. This morning, Mr. X. and I washed all the morning's dishes. I was like going back to childhood, childhood was the simplest time. Society pollutes people's spirits, this collective experience encourages enthusiasm towards culture, it's been a benefit to more or less every "Fairytale" participant.

<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; lived in the U.S.A. for over 10 years, he integrated Chinese and Western culture, from an artistic perspective, he's a harmonious mix of East and West. He can be an artistic consultant to Germany because he knows China's state of affairs, he also understands foreign ways, he can thus straddle the divide and understand the concepts of Western art in a way that a purely Chinese artist is unable to do. It's fitting that the Birds' Nest designers chose &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;to advise on the Chinese side, he has a way of handling things that many other artists can't achieve. There's a multitude of reasons for this: His father was in the Communist Party, a revolutionary, very esteemed, &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;received State Council treatment, he has a tough political background. He studied fine arts, lived in the USA for over ten years, his ideas is a little ahead of his time, all of this contributed to &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;'s achievement. If it hadn't been for that experience living in the US, his art, his line of thought wouldn't be as developed as they are today.

<p><b>
Has anything made a deep impression these past few days in Kassel?</b>
<br>
To be honest, bringing 1001 people over to fully understand the meaning of the pieces in this exhibition, I don't think it's enough, about 10% is pretty good. But it doesn't matter what you say, it's still seeps in, it's a kind of training. These works are really very abstract and modern, very avant-garde, not really art, or even if it is art, people don't necessarily understand it. Foreign and Chinese art have very big conceptual differences, we have a national consciousness, we can't copy foreign contemporary art word for word. Through this project, we can understand the messages in some of the foreign artworks, there are things we don't understand, even after we return we may not have completely refined our understanding. But something unrefined is also art of sorts, to be very philosophical. If something is obvious at first glance, very frank and blunt, I think that fundamentally, it's not art, the visual impact on viewers in undoubtedly weakened.

<p><b>You work in the Commerce and Industry management business, what does that consist of?</b>
<br>
Private firm management, not allowing sales of counterfeit goods, etc. The past few days strolling past shops, I've observed the Germans' manner of conducting business, our managers back home really should learn from them. Here, they are all very polite, they will never stare disdainfully at customers, even if you mess around in their store all day without buying anything, they still won't glare at you, their attitude is very proper.

<p><b>What do you think of &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;'s collapsed installation?</b>

<br>The collapse was a work of art in itself, the piece's collapsed state is also an artwork. Everything is like life, things can't always go smoothly. I think &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;'s art will continue to evolve along a twisting and turning path, but this pressure won't wear him down, his determination is strong. The collapse is in itself a though provoking piece, it doesn't mean that the artist collapsed, rather, it shows that one encounters setbacks on the path of artistic pursuit. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; is still quite young, he's not yet 50 years old, he is slowly building his way, I hope that &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; can keep exploring. I wish him a pleasant journey.

